# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The contact form has two delivery routes. Set ONE of these.
#
# Everything in this file is compiled into the public JavaScript bundle. Vite
# inlines every VITE_* value at build time, so nothing genuinely secret may
# live here. The Gmail app password lives in server/.env, which is read by a
# Node process and never by a browser.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Route A, and the one in use. Generate a key at https://web3forms.com using
# the address you want the mail delivered to; Web3Forms only ever delivers to
# whichever address created the key. Requires no server and nothing to
# maintain.
#
# This key is a public, client-side identifier by design. It is readable in the
# shipped bundle by anyone who opens the page source, and that is expected: it
# identifies a destination, it does not authorise anything. It lives in an env
# var so it can be rotated without editing source, not because it is a secret.
VITE_WEB3FORMS_KEY=

# Route B, the self-hosted mailer in server/. Sends from your own Gmail using
# the template in server/email.mjs, so the mail that arrives is designed rather
# than generic. Takes precedence over the key above when both are set.
#
# It needs a Node process running somewhere reachable over HTTPS, because Gmail
# delivery is SMTP over a raw socket and neither a browser nor an edge runtime
# can open one. Local: http://localhost:8787/api/contact
VITE_CONTACT_ENDPOINT=

# Copy this file to .env.local for local development, AND set the same variable
# in the host's dashboard, because a local-only file never reaches production.
